Document Chunking With Overlap (LangChain RAG Pipeline)
This snippet demonstrates the updated text-splitting phase of a semantic Retrieval-Augmented Generation with LangChain preprocessing pipeline using RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter configured with a sliding overlap window.

Building upon unstructured data workflows (such as long-form podcast transcripts or reference documents), this script breaks down raw corpora into smaller, manageable chunks while preserving contextual continuity across boundaries.

Prerequisites & Dependencies
Python 3.8+

langchain-text-splitters library

Install the required package via pip:

Bash
pip install langchain-text-splitters
Code Overview
Python
from langchain_text_splitters import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter

# Initialize the text splitter with a 1000-character limit and a 200-character overlap
text_splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=1000, chunk_overlap=200)

# Split the loaded documents into overlapping chunks
all_splits = text_splitter.split_documents(docs)

# Output metrics and boundary samples
print(f"Created {len(all_splits)} chunks with overlap")
print(f"\nEnd of first chunk: ...{all_splits[0].page_content[-250:]}")
print(f"\nStart of second chunk: {all_splits[1].page_content[:250]}...")
How It Works
RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter: Recursively splits text along natural boundaries (paragraphs, newlines, sentences, words) to adhere strictly to target character limits without tearing words apart prematurely.

chunk_size=1000: Caps the maximum size of any individual chunk at 1,000 characters to ensure efficient embedding generation and context-window compatibility.

chunk_overlap=200: Introduces a sliding window where adjacent chunks share up to 200 characters of repeated text. This guarantees that thoughts or sentences split near a boundary retain surrounding context in both adjacent chunks, improving semantic search accuracy.

